## Why This Is Asked

Tests for real-time collaboration require multi-user concurrency, deterministic replay of complex conflict resolution, and presence signaling. This question probes the ability to design an end-to-end scenario that exercises OT, WebSocket events, and race-condition handling in a realistic setting.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end multi-user collaboration
- Deterministic OT mock to ensure repeatable runs
- WebSocket presence and real-time cursor synchronization
- Test data isolation and idempotent retries
- Tooling trade-offs between Playwright and Cypress

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch (Playwright):
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('collab editor concurrent edits with OT', async ({ browser }) => {
  const ctx1 = await browser.newContext();
  const page1 = await ctx1.newPage();
  const ctx2 = await browser.newContext();
  const page2 = await ctx2.newPage();
  await page1.goto('/doc/abc');
  await page2.goto('/doc/abc');
  // mock OT engine to deterministic edits
  // trigger concurrent edits and wait for batch window
  // assert final content matches OT-applied result
  // verify presence cursors updated in real time
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate network degradation or WebSocket partitions?
- How would you validate idempotent retries in case of transient OT conflicts?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for multi-tenant isolation are foundational for SaaS apps. The goal is to confirm UI and API boundaries prevent cross-tenant data leakage, a common subtle bug.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenancy data isolation
- Role-based access control (RBAC)
- Deterministic fixtures for repeatable runs
- UI vs API boundary testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Cypress skeleton for tenant isolation
describe('Tenant isolation', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
    seedTenants(['A','B']);
  });
  it('creates user in A and enforces isolation', () => {
    cy.visit('/login');
    cy.login('adminA','A');
    cy.visit('/admin/users');
    cy.get('[data-cy=create-user]').click();
    cy.get('[data-cy=username]').type('newuser');
    cy.get('[data-cy=save]').click();
    cy.contains('newuser').should('exist');
    cy.request({method:'GET', url:'/api/users?tenant=A'}).then(resp => {
      expect(resp.body.some(u => u.username==='newuser')).to.be.true;
    });
    cy.request({method:'GET', url:'/api/users?tenant=B'}).then(resp => {
      expect(resp.body.some(u => u.username==='newuser')).to.be.false;
    });
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this for 100 tenants while keeping tests fast?
- How would you handle flaky network simulations and ensure determinism across runs?

